/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Near an antenna the wavefront arriving from a source is curved, so the test aperture is not lit in phase, the nulls fill in and the gain reads low.
   - R is the distance between the phase centres of the two antennas in m; D is the largest dimension of the test antenna in m (when the source antenna is also large, use the larger of the two, or check both); λ is the wavelength in m.
   - A 0.3 m horn at 10 GHz needs only 6 m; a 3 m dish at 6 GHz needs 360 m, which is why big antennas are measured outdoors or by other means.
   - Reflections that reach the receiver add to or cancel the direct wave and ripple the measured pattern.
   - Gain by substitution (gain transfer): a fixed source antenna transmits.
   - Gtest = Gstd + (Ptest − Pstd)
   - Gtest and Gstd in dBi; Ptest and Pstd in dBm.
   - Two-antenna method: with two identical antennas R apart, the Friis equation gives each gain directly: 2G = Pr − Pt + 20 log(4πR/λ), all in dB.
   - For a pattern cut, the test antenna (usually receiving, by reciprocity the same pattern as transmitting) turns on a positioner about its phase centre while the receiver records power against angle.
   - For polarization, a linear probe is spun about the line joining the antennas.
*/
