/* book Worked example: Q: A 10 GHz test antenna has a largest dimension of 0.3 m. On a range a standard horn of 15 dBi gives a received power of −30 dBm; the test antenna in its place, with the same cable, gives −25 dBm. Find the minimum range length and the test gain. What would the answer be if the test antenna had been connected through an extra adaptor of 0.4 dB loss that was not used with the horn?
   1. λ = 3 × 10⁸/10¹⁰ = 0.03 m.
   2. R ≥ 2D²/λ = 2 × 0.09/0.03 = 6 m between the phase centres.
   3. Ptest − Pstd = −25 − (−30) = +5 dB, so Gtest = 15 + 5 = 20 dBi.
   4. With the extra adaptor, the −25 dBm reading is 0.4 dB lower than the antenna delivered, so the true gain is 20 + 0.4 = 20.4 dBi; ignoring it would under-read by 0.4 dB.
   Answer: R ≥ 6 m and Gtest = 20 dBi; with the uncorrected 0.4 dB adaptor the true gain is 20.4 dBi. */
