/* book Worked example 2: Q: Two identical horns face each other 6 m apart at 10 GHz. The transmitter delivers 0 dBm to one horn and the other receives −30 dBm. Find the gain of each horn. A third horn is then measured with each of them: the pair sums are G1 + G2 = 38.0 dB, G1 + G3 = 35.0 dB and G2 + G3 = 33.0 dB. Find all three gains.
   1. Free-space loss: 20 log(4πR/λ) = 20 log(4π × 6/0.03) = 20 log 2513 = 68.0 dB.
   2. Friis in dB: Pr = Pt + 2G − 68.0, so 2G = −30 − 0 + 68.0 = 38.0 dB and G = 19.0 dBi for each identical horn.
   3. Three-antenna method: add all three sums: 2(G1 + G2 + G3) = 106.0, so the total is 53.0 dB.
   4. G3 = 53.0 − 38.0 = 15.0 dBi; G2 = 53.0 − 35.0 = 18.0 dBi; G1 = 53.0 − 33.0 = 20.0 dBi.
   5. Check: 20.0 + 18.0 = 38.0, 20.0 + 15.0 = 35.0, 18.0 + 15.0 = 33.0.
   Answer: Each identical horn has 19.0 dBi; the three-antenna method gives 20.0, 18.0 and 15.0 dBi without any standard horn. */
