/* book Worked example: Q: An F2 layer has a peak electron density of 1.24 × 10¹² m⁻³ and a virtual height of 300 km. Find (a) the critical frequency, (b) the MUF for stations 1000 km apart, and (c) the skip distance at 15 MHz.
   1. (a) fc = 9√(1.24 × 10¹²) = 9 × 1.1136 × 10⁶ = 10.02 MHz.
   2. (b) D/(2h) = 1000/600 = 1.667; √(1 + 1.667²) = √3.778 = 1.944. MUF = 10.02 × 1.944 = 19.5 MHz.
   3. Equivalently, cos φ = 300/√(300² + 500²) = 300/583.1 = 0.5145, so φ = 59.0° and sec φ = 1.944.
   4. (c) f/fc = 15/10.02 = 1.497; (1.497)² − 1 = 1.240; √1.240 = 1.114. Dskip = 2 × 300 × 1.114 = 668 km.
   5. Optimum working frequency for the 1000 km link: 0.85 × 19.5 = 16.6 MHz.
   Answer: fc ≈ 10.0 MHz; the MUF for 1000 km is about 19.5 MHz (work near 16.6 MHz); at 15 MHz nothing returns closer than about 668 km. */
