/* book exam traps: Writing MUF = fc cos φ. The secant law makes the MUF larger than fc: at φ = 60°, MUF = 2fc, not fc/2. | Calling the MUF the critical frequency. fc is for vertical incidence; the MUF belongs to one oblique path and is always at least fc. | Saying the D layer reflects HF. It absorbs by day; the F2 layer returns long-distance HF. | Using 3.57√h as the radio horizon. That is the geometric horizon; standard refraction gives 4.12√h, with h in metres and d in km. | Expecting a skip zone at frequencies below fc. Below fc even a vertical ray returns, so there is no skip zone. */
